From 27630bcb93a808f093b268ee2c940fa0fb50937f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: kbench <kbench@localhost>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 11:32:54 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] udp: announce a queued batch in one wakeup

__udp_enqueue_schedule_skb() queues up to a NAPI batch of datagrams under
one lock and one rmem accounting update, then calls sk_data_ready() once
per skb:

	while (nb) {
		INDIRECT_CALL_1(READ_ONCE(sk->sk_data_ready),
				sock_def_readable, sk);
		nb--;
	}

The loop is there because the wakeup wakes a single exclusive waiter, so
nb threads each blocked in their own recvmsg() need nb calls.

A consumer on epoll is not that. It registers a non-exclusive waiter, so
every one of those nb calls runs sock_def_readable() in full -- the
smp_mb() inside skwq_has_sleeper(), the waitqueue lock, and
ep_poll_callback(), which observes the same receive queue each time and
has nothing new to report after the first.

That consumer is what a QUIC client is. The transport is in userspace, so
a browser or a runtime speaking HTTP/3 does exactly this: connected UDP
sockets drained from an event loop. TCP does not pay it, because
tcp_data_ready() is gated on tcp_epollin_ready().

Add sock_data_ready_nr(), which states the count once, and
__wake_up_sync_key_nr() under it so the waitqueue can be told how many
exclusive waiters to wake. Exclusive waiters see no change: nb of them
are still woken. The non-exclusive callback runs once instead of nb
times.

sk_data_ready is an overridable pointer, and a socket whose callback has
been replaced -- sockmap, TLS, BPF -- may count something other than
waiters. Those keep the loop; only the default callback takes the new
path.

